   "text": {
    "L1": "A quarter turn is 90 degrees, a half turn is 180 degrees, and a full turn is 360 degrees.",
    "L2": "To measure an angle, put the protractor's centre exactly on the vertex. Keep its baseline along one arm.",
    "L3": "Start with the scale that reads zero on that arm, then read where the other arm crosses.",
    "L4": "Myth: always read the larger number. Not true; the two scales run in opposite directions.",
    "L5": "This angle looks narrow, so its reading must be less than 90 degrees. Choose 40 degrees, not 140 degrees.",
    "L6": "The size of the protractor does not change the angle reading. The same opening gives the same result.",
    "L7": "To draw 70 degrees, draw one arm, centre the protractor on its endpoint, mark 70 on the correct scale, and join.",
    "L8": "Now use the narrow-angle check to choose the correct reading."
   },
